    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Photon external beam therapy is currently the most widely used modality of radiotherapy in the treatment of cancer. However, due to the exponential characteristics of photon attenuation in matter, most of the energy is deposited before the beam even reaches the tumor inside the body. This results in poor dose conformity and large entrance doses, causing undesired irradiation of healthy tissue which can ultimately lead to toxicity and secondary cancers. In this study we describe a new theoretical modality of radiotherapy termed Internal Beam Annihilation Therapy (IBAT) which in an ideal model case can exhibit far superior dose contrast and conformity by using muon anti-muon annihilation at the tumor site. Using Monte Carlo simulation in conjunction with FLUKA, a particle transport code, we evaluate the energy deposition distribution of such a modality and compare its unique characteristics with those of existing modalities.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
